16003,431.440399,48.018799,430.839966,298.727478,430.839966,48.018799,419.420990,298.727478,419.420990,48.018799,418.821533,300.737885,418.821533,48.018799,407.402557,300.737885,407.402557,48.018799,406.859131,298.692505,406.859131,48.018799,395.440155,298.692505,395.440155,48.018799,394.840698,180.455429,394.840698,48.018799,383.421722,180.455429,383.421722" rect="47.661957,383.064880,301.094727,683.196655" subject="Comment on Text" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-16.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>&#xD;</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>By utilizing the coefficients presented in </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#00007E;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Table 2</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>, we can hypothetically calculate that if a team completed 10% more high-speed running than their opposition, they were likely to score an average of 3.2 points more than the opposing team. Nonetheless, we recognize that this is a simple estimation and does not imply cause and effect. However, from a performance perspective in rugby league, while anecdotal, this finding seems logical, i.e., a team that can produce more high-speed running relative to playing time, when compared with their opposition, has a higher likelihood of making a break when in possession of the ball. Furthermore, when in defence, a team that can produce more high-speed running at critical moments may make a try-saving tackle and prevent the opposing team from scoring. Indeed, Gabbett et al (Gabbett &amp; Gahan, </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#00007E;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>2016</span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.7pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>) found that approximately 56% of 2083 repeated high-intensity efforts occurred within 5 min of either scoring or defending a try during 21 semi-professional matches across 11 teams. Together, these data suggest that an increased capacity to perform high-speed running for longer may have performance benefits for elite female rugby league athletes. Furthermore, locomotive movement patterns from single independent teams may have limited use in determining match outcome. Therefore, coaches and sports scientists should consider GNSS-derived movement pattern data as measures of work performed rather than performance.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
